  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, cool, and breezy weather continues through \nThursday. \n\nMore of the same over the next 48 hours as a sprawling high pressure \ncontinues to sit over eastern Canada maintaining cooler than average \nnortheast flow over southern New England. High temperatures each day \nat expected to remain in the low 60s (east coast, closer to the \nonshore wind) to upper 60s (CT Valley). Normal highs for late \nSeptember are 5-10 degrees warmer. As the pressure gradient tightens \nahead of the approaching storms we'll continue to see those winds \nramping up, gusting 25 to 35 mph both Wednesday and Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong coastal low pressure system will bring \nimpacts to southern New England in the form of rain, rough seas, \nstrong winds, and coastal flooding/erosion Friday into the weekend. \nExact timing and magnitude of the winds and rain remain unknown. \n\nModels continue to be consistent in indicating potential for a \nstrong low pressure system to make its way up the eastern seaboard \nand eventually into the Northeast by Friday. Once here, detached \nfrom strong steering flow, the low becomes entrenched overhead, even \nretrograding back to the west for a time. This spells a prolonged \nperiod of wet and windy weather Friday through Sunday, though exact \nplacement and magnitude of impacts remains uncertain. The latest \nensemble probabilities continue to indicate the greatest rainfall \npotential (2+\") over eastern MA and RI. The strongest winds will be \nover the waters where Storm Watches have been hoisted starting on \nFriday. On land the strongest winds will be along the coast and in \nthe higher elevations. Wind headlines will likely be needed as \nensemble guidance indicates >50% chance of gusts over 40 mph, even a \n20-30% chance of gusts nearing 60 mph over Cape Cod and the islands. \nSpecific impacts will come into focus as we learn the eventual track \nof the low. Timing of this early season nor'easter while trees still \nhave all of their leaves will lead to a greater concern for wind \ndamage to trees than if this were to happen late in the fall season. \n\nMinor coastal flooding and significant coastal erosion is also a \nconcern as we experience a prolonged period of gusty NE winds, \ncombined with high astronomical tides. The greatest risk will be for \neastern facing beaches, as early as the Friday afternoon's high tide \nfor Nantucket, and as early as the Friday night high tide in Boston \nHarbor. Many locations will see several tide cycles of minor \nflooding well into the weekend. The low center lingers, weakening, \nSunday before finally exiting east around Monday.",
